<title>BELGIUM: Belgian firm says madcow scare hit H1 profits.</title>
<headline>Belgian firm says madcow scare hit H1 profits.</headline>
<dateline>BRUSSELS 1996-09-13</dateline>
<text>
<p>Belgian food processing firm Ter Beke said on Friday that its margins had been hit by the sharp rise in pig and poultry meat prices that followed the scare over bovine spongiform encephalopathy (BSE), or mad cow disease.</p>
<p>Ham prices have risen 40 percent, it noted, saying that its group net profit for the six months to June 30, 1996 fell 15.5 percent over the year-ago period to 60.1 million Belgian francs ($1.9 million).</p>
<p>&quot;Under market pressures, sales prices have followed suit, but the increase has been sluggish and disproportionate, with the result that margins have narrowed substantially,&quot; the company said in a statement.</p>
<p>It noted its first half figures do not include a number of companies acquired from Unilever Belgium NV on July 1. Another recently acquired company  Heku NV was not bought until July 12, it added.</p>
<p>($ = 31.16 Belgian Francs)</p>
</text>
